A key class pins its hash code to 7. Does the overridden value win?
class Key {
    int id;

    Key(int i) {
        id = i;
    }

    public int hashCode() {
        return 7;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Key k = new Key(1);
        System.out.println(k.hashCode() == 7);
    }
}

Both tag keys report the same hash code but compare unequal. What does the map print?
class Tag {
    String name;

    Tag(String s) {
        name = s;
    }

    public int hashCode() {
        return 1;
    }

    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Tag && ((Tag) o).name.equals(name);
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Map<Tag, String> m = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        m.put(new Tag("a"), "A");
        m.put(new Tag("b"), "B");
        System.out.println(m.get(new Tag("a")) + " " + m.size());
    }
}

A key is stored, then its field is changed. Which lookups can still find it?
class Key {
    int code;

    Key(int c) {
        code = c;
    }

    public int hashCode() {
        return code;
    }

    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        return o instanceof Key && ((Key) o).code == code;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Map<Key, String> m = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        Key k = new Key(1);
        m.put(k, "one");
        k.code = 9;
        System.out.println(m.get(new Key(1)) + " " + m.get(new Key(9)) + " " + m.size());
    }
}

